"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative findings included a high baseline PSA of 86 and a pathological stage of pT4b with extraprostatic extension, positive surgical margins, and seminal vesicle invasion. The presence of lymph node metastasis was also noted. The biopsy revealed a higher grade of adenocarcinoma (Gleason 4+4) compared to the prostatectomy specimen (Gleason 4+3). These aggressive features contribute to a higher calculated risk. Countervailing factors include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and the absence of documented recurrence. Nodal status was documented as positive metastasis. The difference between the biopsy and prostatectomy histology introduces uncertainty regarding the true extent of the disease. Postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory are critical unknowns that would significantly revise this prognosis. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data prevents a definitive assessment of disease control. The fixed estimated horizon is 30.87 months. This is a predicted recurrence horizon, not evidence of observed recurrence. The sensitivity range is 24.70–37.05 months; its coverage has not been validated."
